2. Executive Summary & Project Abstract
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
BloodLink is a production-grade, life-critical regional blood requisition and voluntary donor allocation engine
designed to overcome the critical logistical, computational, and clinical challenges associated with blood cold-chain
perishability across Gujarat's 34 administrative districts. Blood components possess strict biological expiration
windows: whole blood degrades in 35–42 days, platelets degrade within 5 days, and plasma requires sub-zero
cryopreservation. Traditional centralized healthcare portals suffer from linear database table scans, lack of concurrency
buffering, and manual donor lookup phone trees that waste critical resuscitation minutes.
This report details the architectural design, mathematical formulation, algorithmic implementation, and empirical
verification of the Four Core Data Structures that power BloodLink:
1. In-Memory 3D Static Matrix Indexing: Partitions statewide registered voluntary donors across three contiguous
orthogonal axes: `City × BloodGroup × MaxHeap`. Provides strict O(1) indexing for instant donor pool access.
2. Binary Max-Heap Priority Queue: Operates inside every matrix leaf to rank voluntary donors via multi-factor
clinical scoring (exact match $+100$, compatible match $+60$, first-time $+20$, optimal age $+20$), enabling O(log
N) top-candidate extraction.
3. Linear FIFO Request Queue & WebSocket Broker: High-throughput ingestion buffer that serializes incoming ICU
emergency requisitions, generates unique atomic audit tokens, and prevents race conditions under mass-casualty
surges.
4. Separate Chaining Hash Table with Polynomial Rolling Hash: Pre-indexes 316+ Gujarat cities into 128 buckets
across 1,350 multi-length prefixes, executing sub-millisecond client-side autocomplete search in O(1) average time.
