3. System Architecture & Problem Formulation
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
3.1 Regional Cold-Chain Perishability & Gujarat District Logistics
Gujarat spans 34 administrative districts, ranging from hyper-dense metropolitan healthcare hubs (Ahmedabad, Surat,
Vadodara) to geographically vast rural and desert regions (Kutch, Banaskantha, Dang). Emergency healthcare delivery
across this regional terrain faces severe cold-chain constraints:
• Perishable Degradation: Platelets have a maximum shelf life of 5 days under continuous agitation at 20–24°C.
Packed Red Blood Cells (PRBC) expire in 35–42 days at 2–6°C. Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) requires cryopreservation
below -18°C.
• Severe Supply-Demand Asymmetries: Advanced cardiac and trauma centers in urban districts experience rapid
inventory depletion, while peripheral district hospitals often hold unutilized units that risk spoilage.
• Fatal Database Latency: Traditional relational databases perform linear scans or disk-bound index lookups. In acute
trauma hemorrhage, a patient can exsanguinate in less than 7 minutes; relying on slow database queries during
emergency triage directly costs human lives.
3.2 Integrated 4-Data-Structure Pipeline Architecture
BloodLink replaces ad-hoc querying with an integrated, multi-tier data structure pipeline designed for deterministic
execution:
1. Separate Chaining Hash Table (Client Autocomplete): When a dispatcher types a city in the requisition form, the
client-side hash table matches prefix keys in $O(1)$ average time across 316+ Gujarat cities.
2. FIFO Request Queue (WebSocket Ingestion Broker): Upon submission, the request is ingested via Socket.io into a
linear FIFO queue, stamped with an atomic token (`TOKEN-XXXX-XXX`), and buffered to eliminate race conditions.
3. In-Memory 3D Static Matrix (Donor Pool Index): The triage processor jumps directly in $O(1)$ constant time to
donors[City][BloodGroup] without scanning relational tables.
4. Binary Max-Heap Priority Queue (Standby Donor Ranking): The leaf cell's Binary Max-Heap extracts the top-3
voluntary backup donors in $O(\log N)$ time, prioritized by clinical cross-match compatibility and donor eligibility.
